  "live_records": {
    "q-149691": {
      "dom_id": "step-q149691",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=2\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)}{(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\pi  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225095/assets/13bb063a7ae79887a653e5f1c330851e56183049f77853a6a1d35d4a023d615c.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
