    },
    "q-103815": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103815",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0\\cdot (x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225095/assets/6b8602110483fcc09d9ac0c04a16ab4e624f00a2ff752851e9c027bef5c9bc83.png)",
