    "q-351547": {
      "dom_id": "step-q351547",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find the Cartesian equation of the normal line to the polar curve $r=4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line is $y=$\n{{field-1}} $x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})-4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}\\,x+\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225095/assets/2bb2678804e79eb60cf9e1b16df60b8dad4ec4ccf8b2a9234a838c394ab9f3ac.png)",
