      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r={\\theta }^{2}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0}{(2\\cdot 0-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225095/assets/8f39669eaedc42a26d28d96f6dbb961080816e7fadd182b79a260c8de27b3d1a.png)",
